window.ALFORD_EXPLORE = {"version":2,"people":[{"id":"john","name":"John Seaborn Alford","dates":"1807–1891 or 1897","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandfather","group":"line","summary":"John was a Washington Parish farmer and Christine’s great-grandfather. He married Margaret in 1833, and they raised a large family. Their land lay near the Bogue Chitto River; one of John’s lots later contained the family cemetery.","body":["John was a Washington Parish farmer and Christine’s great-grandfather. He married Margaret in 1833, and they raised a large family. Their land lay near the Bogue Chitto River; one of John’s lots later contained the family cemetery.","He had a twin brother, Seaborn John, who became a Methodist preacher. John’s own son was also named Seaborn. Census and family accounts support the relationship from John and Margaret to this younger Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.","The twins’ later family account names their father Jacob, but we have not securely connected him to Julius Alford’s son in North Carolina. John’s death year is also unsettled: later sources give 1891 and 1897."],"records":["twin","parentage","margaret","patents","field","farm","landscape"],"passages":["twins","john-land","farm"],"places":["bogue-chitto","family-cemetery"],"refs":"C-140, C-209; S-1134, S-1643, S-1670, S-1869","events":[]},{"id":"margaret","name":"Margaret Brumfield Alford","dates":"1819–1885","relationship":"Christine’s great-grandmother","group":"line","summary":"Margaret was John Alford’s wife and Christine’s great-grandmother. Her church obituary says they married in 1833, when she was about fourteen. At her death in 1885, it counted a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.","body":["Margaret was John Alford’s wife and Christine’s great-grandmother. Her church obituary says they married in 1833, when she was about fourteen. At her death in 1885, it counted a husband, fourteen surviving children and forty-three grandchildren.","She joined the Methodists in 1853, was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and later joined Bogue Chitto church. These changes in membership are among the few details we have of her life beyond her family.","Her maiden name, Brumfield, comes from later family compilations; the church obituary calls her Margaret Alford. Census and family records support John and Margaret as the parents of Seaborn, Christine’s grandfather.","A 1965 cemetery survey lists Margaret beside J. S. Alford in the family burial ground in Section 18, near John’s land."],"records":["margaret","parentage","landscape"],"passages":["margaret","adult-household"],"places":["bogue-chitto","family-cemetery"],"refs":"C-209; S-1826, S-1869; S-1857","events":[],"graphicName":"Margaret Alford"},{"id":"seaborn","name":"Seaborn L. Alford","dates":"1844–1919","relationship":"Christine’s grandfather","group":"line","summary":"Seaborn was a farmer near Warnerton and Christine’s grandfather. Census and family accounts identify him as John and Margaret’s son. He served in the Confederate cavalry, was captured at Port Hudson in 1863 and was released on parole.","body":["Seaborn was a farmer near Warnerton and Christine’s grandfather. Census and family accounts identify him as John and Margaret’s son. He served in the Confederate cavalry, was captured at Port Hudson in 1863 and was released on parole.","He married Laura Ellzey in 1866. They had ten children, according to his obituary; Esco, Christine’s father, was one of them. After Laura’s death, Seaborn married Candacy Bankston and had three more children.","His homestead lay south of his father’s land. When he died in 1919, his obituary said his home was “in sight of the place of his birth.”"],"records":["seabornObit","parentage","muster","parole","homestead","escoParentage"],"passages":["son","port-hudson","homestead","candacy"],"places":["bogue-chitto","port-hudson","family-cemetery"],"refs":"C-190, C-209, C-211, C-212; S-1292, S-1654","events":[]},{"id":"laura","name":"Laura A. Ellzey Alford","dates":"1844–1903, reported","relationship":"Christine’s grandmother","group":"line","summary":"Laura was Seaborn Alford’s first wife, Esco’s mother and Christine’s grandmother. She and Seaborn married on Christmas Eve, 1866, and had ten children, according to his later obituary.","body":["Laura was Seaborn Alford’s first wife, Esco’s mother and Christine’s grandmother. She and Seaborn married on Christmas Eve, 1866, and had ten children, according to his later obituary.","She was probably the daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey. Her surname appears in several spellings; Seaborn’s obituary calls her Laura A. Ellzy.","Laura died in 1903, after more than thirty-six years of marriage. Her gravestone is shown in the story, but its dates are difficult to read. The dates used here come from an earlier cemetery transcription, with her birth date also supported by a family Bible abstract."],"records":["laura","seabornObit","escoParentage","ellzeyBible"],"passages":["laura","laura-death"],"places":["bogue-chitto","family-cemetery"],"refs":"C-191, C-212, C-213; S-1283, S-1654, S-1826","events":[]},{"id":"esco","name":"Esco Seaborn Alford","dates":"1878–1958","relationship":"Christine’s father","group":"line","summary":"Esco was Christine’s father, a farmer and dairyman near Mount Hermon. He was the son of Seaborn and Laura Alford. He also served his community as a church deacon and parish jury commissioner.","body":["Esco was Christine’s father, a farmer and dairyman near Mount Hermon. He was the son of Seaborn and Laura Alford. He also served his community as a church deacon and parish jury commissioner.","He married Mary Lou Fussell in 1899. By 1900 they had their own farm household, near other members of his family. Their daughter Christine later trained as a nurse in New Orleans.","After Mary Lou died in 1946, he married Lynnie Schilling, who died in 1947, and then Florence Bennett in 1951. He died after a stroke in April 1958 and was buried at Bogue Chitto Baptist Church."],"records":["escoDraft","escoParentage","escoMarriage","escoObit","escoPortrait","census1900","nursing","lynnieMarriage"],"passages":["esco","retirement","esco-death"],"places":["bogue-chitto","church-cemetery"],"refs":"C-190, C-191, C-208; S-1292, S-1805, S-1842","events":[]},{"id":"mary-lou","name":"Mary Lou Fussell Alford","dates":"1883 or 1884–1946","relationship":"Christine’s mother","group":"line","summary":"Mary Lou Fussell Alford, also called Lou, was Christine’s mother. She married Esco in December 1899, and they made their home in the farming community near Mount Hermon. In 1930, their household included sixteen-year-old Tolbert and nine-year-old Christine.","body":["Mary Lou Fussell Alford, also called Lou, was Christine’s mother. She married Esco in December 1899, and they made their home in the farming community near Mount Hermon. In 1930, their household included sixteen-year-old Tolbert and nine-year-old Christine.","Her father was Henry T. Fussell. Eliza or Liza Hobbs is her likely mother, although the death indexes disagree on the mother’s name. Her own birth year is also uncertain: records give 1883 and 1884.","She died at home on 4 February 1946, three days after Christine’s marriage date. Her obituary names her daughter as “Mrs. Christine Knight.”"],"records":["escoMarriage","maryObit","census1930","census1900","marriage","fussellFamily"],"passages":["mary-lou","mary-lou-death"],"places":["bogue-chitto","church-cemetery"],"refs":"C-186, C-208; S-1277, S-1814","events":[]},{"id":"christine","name":"Christine Alford Knight","dates":"About 1921–2008","relationship":"The descendant followed in this story","group":"line","summary":"Christine grew up on a farm near Mount Hermon, the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in 1941 and was working in public health in Baton Rouge by 1942.","body":["Christine grew up on a farm near Mount Hermon, the daughter of Esco and Mary Lou Alford. She graduated from Touro Infirmary’s nursing school in 1941 and was working in public health in Baton Rouge by 1942.","She married Charles Binford Knight in 1946 and continued nursing. Her work included coordinating clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes. She retired in 1975 after thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department, according to her obituary.","She died in 2008, having outlived Charles. Her later obituary gives her birth as 1 January 1921; an original birth record has not been found in this research. Accounts disagree on her exact death date."],"records":["census1930","nursing","directory","marriage","census1950","memorial"],"passages":["christine","graduation","baton-rouge"],"places":["touro","baton-rouge"],"refs":"C-112, C-113, C-184; S-1017, S-1861","events":[]},{"id":"charles","name":"Charles Binford Knight","dates":"Married 1946","relationship":"Christine’s husband","group":"relatives","summary":"Charles Binford Knight was Christine’s husband. They married in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946 and later lived in Baton Rouge.","body":["Charles Binford Knight was Christine’s husband. They married in Bienville Parish on 1 February 1946 and later lived in Baton Rouge.","In 1950, he was an assistant manager at a funeral home while Christine worked as a parish health nurse. Her obituary describes a forty-four-year marriage and says that he died before her."],"records":["marriage","census1950","memorial"],"passages":["christine-marriage","baton-rouge"],"places":["baton-rouge"],"refs":"C-113, C-172; S-0993, S-1861","events":[]},{"id":"jacob-twins-father","name":"Jacob Alford, reported father of the twins","dates":"Death date unknown","relationship":"Reported father of John S. and Seaborn J.","group":"earlier","summary":"The twins’ 1884 obituary names their father Jacob and says he came from South Carolina before their 1807 birth.","body":["The obituary was written long after the family’s move. It does not name the twins’ mother or connect this Jacob to Julius Alford’s North Carolina son or to the 1831 Pearl River claimant.","The account says the boys were quite small when their father died. That memory is in tension with a separate printed register’s 1824 death for another Jacob candidate; the two names are not merged here."],"records":["twin"],"passages":["twins","survey"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-140; S-1134; compare C-139, C-170; S-1130, S-1227, S-1572","events":[{"year":"1807","label":"Twins reportedly born near modern Osyka","passage":"twins","record":"twin"}]},{"id":"seaborn-j-twin","name":"Seaborn John Alford","dates":"1807–1884","relationship":"John S. Alford’s twin brother","group":"relatives","summary":"Seaborn J. became a Methodist local preacher; his 1884 obituary names John S. as his twin.","body":["An obituary by W. H. Germany in the New Orleans Christian Advocate dates his birth to 11 October 1807 near modern Osyka and his death to 7 February 1884. It supplies the father Jacob story but is a late informant for 1807 events.","Seaborn John, the twin, is a different person from Seaborn L., John’s son born in 1844."],"records":["twin"],"passages":["twins"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-140; S-1134","events":[{"year":"1807","label":"Twin birth reported","record":"twin"},{"year":"1884","label":"Death reported in obituary","record":"twin"}]},{"id":"james-st-peters","name":"James Alford of St. Peter’s Parish","dates":"Dates unknown","relationship":"Father of Julius in the parish register","group":"branches","summary":"The printed St. Peter’s Parish register names James as father of a Julius born in September 1717.","body":["The parish entries also name sons James and Warren. No reviewed entry names their mother. The proposed father of James, John Alford Sr., remains unproved.","This is an explicit deeper path into the North Carolina household. The connection from that household to Christine’s Louisiana line remains unproved."],"records":["juliusBackground"],"passages":["beginning"],"places":["tar-river"],"refs":"C-146; S-1568, S-1689, S-1711; docs/research-intake/alford-colonial-lives-and-origins-2026-09-07/ADJUDICATION.md","events":[]},{"id":"julius-bute","name":"Julius Alford of Bute County","dates":"Reported 1717–by 1771","relationship":"North Carolina testator and father of Jacob of Franklin County","group":"earlier","summary":"Julius’s 1768 Bute County will names his wife Lucy and seven children, including Jacob.","body":["He devised his home plantation, on both sides of a river read with uncertainty as Tar or Tarr, to his son Jacob. His will also names sons John, Isaac, Goodrich and Job, and daughters Patty and Sarah.","The identification of this testator with the Julius born to James in Virginia’s St. Peter’s Parish is strong but indirect. It does not identify his son with the Louisiana twins’ father."],"records":["juliusBackground","will"],"passages":["beginning","will"],"places":["tar-river"],"refs":"P-047; C-139, C-146; S-1130, S-1568, S-1711","events":[{"year":"1768","label":"Will written in Bute County","passage":"will","record":"will"},{"year":"1771","label":"Will proved after death","record":"will"}]},{"id":"jacob-north-carolina","name":"Jacob Alford, Julius’s North Carolina son","dates":"Dates unknown","relationship":"Documented son of Julius; Louisiana identity unresolved","group":"earlier","summary":"Jacob named himself as the son of deceased Julius Alford in his 1789 Franklin County deed.","body":["Julius’s will had given Jacob the home plantation. North Carolina deeds follow a Jacob from inherited Tar River land toward Cumberland County by 1790. The Cumberland transactions are a probable continuation, though they do not restate his parentage.","No reviewed record joins this man to the twins born in Louisiana in 1807. The proposed North Carolina–Georgia–Louisiana route remains a series of identity judgments, not one documented itinerary."],"records":["will","deed","ncMove"],"passages":["will","deed","cumberland"],"places":["tar-river","cumberland"],"refs":"C-139; S-1130, S-1131, S-1536, S-1608, S-1743, S-1746","events":[{"year":"1789","label":"Calls himself Julius’s son","passage":"deed","record":"deed"},{"year":"1790","label":"Cumberland residence in Franklin deed","passage":"cumberland","record":"ncMove"}]},{"id":"john-alford-senior","name":"John Alford Sr. of St. Peter’s Parish","dates":"Died 1709/10, reported","relationship":"Proposed earlier relative; no proven parent edge","group":"branches","summary":"Parish material reports John Alford Sr.’s death on 14 March 1709/10.","body":["He is a proposed father for James of St. Peter’s, based on place and timing, without a record naming the relationship. Earlier Warwick, York and New Kent Johns, and the 1726 testator John, cannot simply be merged with him."],"records":["juliusBackground"],"passages":["beginning"],"places":[],"refs":"docs/research-intake/alford-colonial-lives-and-origins-2026-09-07/ADJUDICATION.md; S-1568, S-1689","events":[]},{"id":"jacob-printed-register","name":"Jacob Alford of the printed Bible register","dates":"1761–1824, as printed","relationship":"Named father of Nedom Judge Alford","group":"branches","summary":"A 1968 newspaper rendering of a copied Bible gives Jacob’s birth and death and names Nedom as his son.","body":["The original Bible, its handwriting and the intermediate copy have not been examined. The printed relationship labels may be editorial additions.","Its 1824 death sits awkwardly with the 1884 recollection that the 1807 twins were small when their father died. The register never names those twins."],"records":["jacobFamily"],"passages":["jacob-family"],"places":["cumberland"],"refs":"S-1572; docs/research-intake/needham-bible-newspaper-recovery-1968-2026-09-07/review/bible-transcription.md","events":[{"year":"1824","label":"Death as printed in 1968 register","record":"jacobFamily"}]},{"id":"elizabeth-bryant","name":"Elizabeth Bryant Alford","dates":"Born 1765, as printed; death unknown","relationship":"Named wife of register Jacob and mother of Nedom","group":"branches","summary":"The printed Bible register calls Elizabeth Bryant Jacob’s wife and Nedom Judge Alford’s mother.","body":["Her birth date of 20 June 1765 is the newspaper’s reading of a copied Bible. No original marriage record was inspected for this proposal, and her later fate is unresolved."],"records":["jacobFamily"],"passages":["jacob-family"],"places":[],"refs":"S-1572; docs/research-intake/needham-bible-newspaper-recovery-1968-2026-09-07/review/bible-transcription.md","events":[]},{"id":"nedom-judge","name":"Nedom Judge Alford","dates":"1789–1869, as printed","relationship":"Son of Jacob and Elizabeth in printed register","group":"branches","summary":"The 1968 register names Nedom’s parents and gives his birth as 12 July 1789.","body":["The same newspaper continuation prints his death as 19 September 1869 and records a marriage to Martha Waddill. These are copied Bible readings, not original entries. “Nedom” is retained as the printed spelling; “Needham” is a later normalization."],"records":["jacobFamily"],"passages":["jacob-family"],"places":[],"refs":"S-1572; docs/research-intake/needham-bible-newspaper-recovery-1968-2026-09-07/review/bible-transcription.md","events":[]},{"id":"candacy-bankston","name":"Candacy Bankston Alford","dates":"About 1880–1955","relationship":"Seaborn L.’s second wife; Esco’s stepmother","group":"relatives","summary":"Candacy married Seaborn L. after Laura’s death and was mother of Jacob, Frankie and Agnes.","body":["Seaborn’s 1919 obituary gives their marriage date as 8 September 1909 and counts three children. Candacy’s contemporary 1955 death notice names the children and identifies four surviving stepchildren. Her own exact birth date is not established."],"records":["seabornObit","candacyObit"],"passages":["candacy"],"places":["bogue-chitto","church-cemetery"],"refs":"C-210, C-212; S-1652, S-1654","events":[{"year":"1909","label":"Marriage date reported by Seaborn’s obituary","passage":"candacy","record":"seabornObit"},{"year":"1955","label":"Death notice names three children","record":"candacyObit"}]},{"id":"henry-t-fussell","name":"Henry T. Fussell","dates":"About 1857–1940","relationship":"Mary Lou’s father","group":"relatives","summary":"Henry’s obituary and the family’s own card of thanks name Mrs. E. S. Alford among his children.","body":["His 1940 death index names John Fussell and Sarah Corkin as parents; the near-age “Harvey” in the 1860 household is an inference, not a corrected spelling of the manuscript.","He was buried in an Alford cemetery. A Henry Fussell patented nearby land in 1895; identification of that patentee with Mary Lou’s father is plausible but unproved."],"records":["maryObit","patents","fussellFamily","jepthaObit"],"passages":["mary-lou","township"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-186, C-187; S-1277, S-1278, S-1296, S-1810, S-1857","events":[]},{"id":"eliza-hobbs","name":"Eliza or Liza Hobbs","dates":"Dates unknown","relationship":"Supported mother of Mary Lou","group":"relatives","summary":"Mary Lou’s death index names her mother Eliza Hobs; two relatives’ derivative records say Liza Hobbs.","body":["A third sibling’s index instead says Sara Jane Hobs. The original death certificates remain unread. An Eliza in an earlier Mississippi household is only a candidate, and no parent edge to William Hobbs or Mary Willoughby is proposed."],"records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"passages":["mary-lou"],"places":[],"refs":"C-186, C-188; S-1277, S-1293, S-1296","events":[]},{"id":"john-fussell","name":"John Fussell","dates":"About 1796–after 1870","relationship":"Reported father of Henry T. Fussell","group":"branches","summary":"Henry’s death index names John Fussell as father, and a John appears in the 1860 and 1870 Washington Parish households.","body":["The 1860 household has a boy written Harvey, while the 1870 household has Henry; equating them is a supported inference. John’s own birthplace is listed as Georgia in the censuses."],"records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"passages":["mary-lou"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-187; S-1277, S-1278","events":[]},{"id":"sarah-corkern","name":"Sarah Corkin or Corkern","dates":"About 1811–after 1860","relationship":"Reported mother of Henry T. Fussell","group":"branches","summary":"Henry’s 1940 death index names Sarah Corkin as his mother.","body":["She appears in the 1860 John Fussell household. The surname spelling varies; the 1870 census no longer lists her there and does not establish a death date."],"records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"passages":["mary-lou"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-187; S-1277, S-1278","events":[]},{"id":"john-ellzey","name":"John Ellzey Sr.","dates":"About 1797–after 1880","relationship":"Supported father of Laura A. Ellzey Alford","group":"branches","summary":"Laura appears as a child in John Ellzey’s Pike County households in both 1850 and 1860.","body":["A later Bible abstract gives Laura’s exact birth date matching her surveyed stone, and supports identifying that child with Seaborn’s wife. No contemporary record read here explicitly calls Laura Alford John’s daughter. Her 1900 census puts her father’s birthplace in Alabama, conflicting with John’s South Carolina birth entries."],"records":["laura","seabornObit","ellzeyBible"],"passages":["laura","south-carolina"],"places":[],"refs":"C-213; S-1283, S-1654, S-1826","events":[]},{"id":"elizabeth-coney","name":"Elizabeth Coney Ellzey","dates":"Dates unknown","relationship":"Supported mother of Laura A. Ellzey Alford","group":"branches","summary":"A Bible abstract calls Laura a child of Elizabeth and John Ellzey; an 1823 marriage index supplies the Coney surname.","body":["Elizabeth is in John’s 1850 household, but her recorded age of 34 conflicts with a marriage twenty-seven years earlier. Laura’s own Bible entry is not on the photographed original leaf."],"records":["laura","ellzeyBible"],"passages":["laura"],"places":[],"refs":"C-213; S-1283, S-1826","events":[]},{"id":"sarah-alford-brumfield","name":"Sarah (Alford) Ellzey Brumfield","dates":"About 1842–1929","relationship":"John and Margaret’s daughter; Seaborn L.’s sister","group":"relatives","summary":"Sarah’s own widow applications name Jesse C. Ellzey and Daniel E. Brumfield as her husbands.","body":["A 1929 affidavit by Jeptha M. Alford calls her his sister and says she died at his home without descendant heirs. A 1929 newspaper notice also calls her his sister.","Her first marriage date is disputed even within her 1927 applications: about 1872 versus an overwritten October 1868. June 1870 households place both parties with their birth families. Her burial is unlocated in the held survey corpus."],"records":["parentage","sarahPension"],"passages":["adult-household"],"places":[],"refs":"S-1853, S-1834, S-1643; ledger/searches.md S-1853; docs/research-intake/alford-story-2026-09-22","events":[{"year":"1927","label":"Widow applications name both husbands","record":"sarahPension"}]},{"id":"jeptha-m-alford","name":"Jeptha Martin Alford","dates":"About 1862–1948","relationship":"John and Margaret’s son; Sarah’s brother","group":"relatives","summary":"Jeptha lived near the Alford homeplace and was named by Sarah as her brother.","body":["His 1948 obituary says he was born within 300 yards of the home where he died. His own affidavit for Sarah’s estate identifies her as his sister. A cemetery survey’s “John Griff” father wording conflicts with the other John S. evidence and should not be silently corrected."],"records":["patents","parentage","jepthaObit"],"passages":["john-land"],"places":[],"refs":"S-1810, S-1853, S-1856, S-1857","events":[{"year":"1948","label":"Death at home, per obituary","record":"jepthaObit"}]},{"id":"lynnie-schilling","name":"Lynnie Schilling Simmons Alford","dates":"About 1879–1947","relationship":"Esco’s later wife","group":"relatives","summary":"A death index identifies Lynnie as Esco Alford’s wife after Mary Lou’s death.","body":["The index names parents Esco Schillings and Elizabeth Ellzey. Linking her mother to Laura’s sister Eveline is a plausible family reconstruction, not a proven first-cousin relationship to Esco. Lynnie died in October 1947."],"records":["escoObit","lynnieMarriage"],"passages":["lynnie"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"S-1828, S-1833, S-1842; C-213","events":[{"year":"1946","label":"Indexed marriage to Esco","record":"lynnieMarriage"}]},{"id":"barksdale-brumfield","name":"Barksdale W. Brumfield","dates":"Dates unknown","relationship":"Neighbouring section 19 patentee","group":"neighbours","summary":"Barksdale W. Brumfield received a section 19 patent on the same day as John S. Alford’s 1858 entries.","body":["His surname alone does not establish kinship with Margaret Brumfield. He is a land neighbour and map label, not an ancestor in this presentation tree."],"records":["patents","plat"],"passages":["township"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"S-1810; atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §6","events":[]},{"id":"lucy-bute","name":"Lucy Alford","dates":"Alive in 1768","relationship":"Wife of Julius of Bute County","group":"earlier","summary":"Julius’s 1768 will names Lucy as his wife.","body":["The will grants her a horse, saddle, bed and use of residual property during her lifetime or widowhood. No maiden name or later death date is established here."],"records":["will"],"passages":["will"],"places":["tar-river"],"refs":"S-1130","events":[]},{"id":"jacob-claimant","name":"Jacob Alford, Pearl River land claimant","dates":"Settlement claim from December 1807; dead by 20 January 1831","relationship":"Land claimant; relationship to the twins unproved","group":"earlier","summary":"A federal list records one Jacob Alford settlement claim west of the Pearl River, and a later survey names his heirs without listing them.","body":["James O. Cosby’s list of actual settlers, certified 7 June 1813, records Jacob Alford with one claim by settlement beginning in December 1807. The settlement month need not mark his first physical arrival.","A survey made for Jacob Alford’s heirs on 20 January 1831 measures 636.54 acres along the Bogue Chitto and cites certificate 41. The later resurvey connects it to Cosby claim 987. These are successive measurements of one claim; the survey proves the claimant had died by January 1831 but does not name any individual heir.","The claimant’s identification with the twins’ father or with Julius’s North Carolina son is plausible but unproved. No parent, spouse or possible-identity edge is proposed."],"records":["settlement","survey"],"passages":["settlement","survey"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"refs":"C-170; S-1135, S-1227, S-1798","events":[{"year":"1807","label":"Settlement month recorded in Cosby list","record":"settlement","passage":"settlement"},{"year":"1813","label":"Cosby list certified","record":"settlement"},{"year":"1831","label":"Survey for unnamed heirs","record":"survey","passage":"survey"}]}],"edges":[{"id":"james-julius","from":"james-st-peters","to":"julius-bute","kind":"parent","label":"James → Julius","qualification":"Parish register states father of the 1717 child; identification of that child with Bute testator is strong but indirect.","records":["juliusBackground"],"refs":"C-146; S-1568, S-1689"},{"id":"julius-jacob-nc","from":"julius-bute","to":"jacob-north-carolina","kind":"parent","label":"Julius → Jacob","qualification":"Will names son Jacob; the 1789 grantee independently calls himself Julius’s son.","records":["will","deed"],"refs":"C-139; S-1130, S-1131"},{"id":"julius-lucy","from":"julius-bute","to":"lucy-bute","kind":"spouse","label":"Julius and Lucy","qualification":"The will names Lucy as wife. Lucy is a linked brief profile.","records":["will"],"refs":"S-1130"},{"id":"jacob-register-elizabeth","from":"jacob-printed-register","to":"elizabeth-bryant","kind":"spouse","label":"Jacob and Elizabeth","qualification":"Named as spouses in a 1968 newspaper copy of a family Bible; original Bible not examined.","records":["jacobFamily"],"refs":"S-1572"},{"id":"jacob-register-nedom","from":"jacob-printed-register","to":"nedom-judge","kind":"parent","label":"Jacob → Nedom","qualification":"Printed register’s relationship label, potentially introduced during copying.","records":["jacobFamily"],"refs":"S-1572"},{"id":"elizabeth-nedom","from":"elizabeth-bryant","to":"nedom-judge","kind":"parent","label":"Elizabeth → Nedom","qualification":"Printed register’s relationship label, potentially introduced during copying.","records":["jacobFamily"],"refs":"S-1572"},{"id":"jacob-twins-john","from":"jacob-twins-father","to":"john","kind":"parent","label":"Jacob → John S.","qualification":"Father named by John’s twin in a late 1884 obituary; identity beyond this role unknown.","records":["twin"],"refs":"C-140; S-1134"},{"id":"jacob-twins-seaborn","from":"jacob-twins-father","to":"seaborn-j-twin","kind":"parent","label":"Jacob → Seaborn J.","qualification":"Father named in Seaborn J.’s 1884 obituary; identity beyond this role unknown.","records":["twin"],"refs":"C-140; S-1134"},{"id":"john-seaborn-twins","from":"john","to":"seaborn-j-twin","kind":"association","label":"Twin brothers","qualification":"Their twin relationship is given in Seaborn J.’s obituary.","records":["twin"],"refs":"C-140; S-1134"},{"id":"john-margaret","from":"john","to":"margaret","kind":"spouse","label":"John and Margaret","qualification":"Margaret’s 1885 church obituary reports a marriage in 1833.","records":["margaret"],"refs":"S-1869"},{"id":"john-seaborn","from":"john","to":"seaborn","kind":"parent","label":"John → Seaborn L.","qualification":"Supported by household placements and later family testimony; no near-birth relationship entry.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"C-209; S-1643, S-1132"},{"id":"margaret-seaborn","from":"margaret","to":"seaborn","kind":"parent","label":"Margaret → Seaborn L.","qualification":"Supported by household placements and later family testimony; no near-birth relationship entry.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"C-209; S-1643, S-1132"},{"id":"john-sarah-alford-brumfield","from":"john","to":"sarah-alford-brumfield","kind":"parent","label":"John → Sarah","qualification":"Supported by childhood household and later death-index parent naming.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"S-1643, S-1828, S-1853"},{"id":"margaret-sarah-alford-brumfield","from":"margaret","to":"sarah-alford-brumfield","kind":"parent","label":"Margaret → Sarah","qualification":"Supported by childhood household and later death-index parent naming.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"S-1643, S-1828, S-1853"},{"id":"john-jeptha-m-alford","from":"john","to":"jeptha-m-alford","kind":"parent","label":"John → Jeptha","qualification":"Supported by death-index parent naming; later survey father name conflicts.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"S-1828, S-1857"},{"id":"margaret-jeptha-m-alford","from":"margaret","to":"jeptha-m-alford","kind":"parent","label":"Margaret → Jeptha","qualification":"Supported by death-index parent naming and collateral family evidence.","records":["parentage"],"refs":"S-1828, S-1853"},{"id":"seaborn-laura","from":"seaborn","to":"laura","kind":"spouse","label":"Seaborn and Laura","qualification":"1919 obituary reports 24 December 1866; original marriage return unreached.","records":["seabornObit"],"refs":"C-212; S-1654"},{"id":"seaborn-candacy","from":"seaborn","to":"candacy-bankston","kind":"spouse","label":"Seaborn and Candacy","qualification":"1919 obituary reports 8 September 1909; 1955 notice confirms their three children.","records":["seabornObit","candacyObit"],"refs":"C-210, C-212; S-1652, S-1654"},{"id":"seaborn-esco","from":"seaborn","to":"esco","kind":"parent","label":"Seaborn → Esco","qualification":"1880 census explicitly calls Escoe son of Seaborn.","records":["census1880"],"refs":"C-190; S-1292"},{"id":"laura-esco","from":"laura","to":"esco","kind":"parent","label":"Laura → Esco","qualification":"Supported by wife/mother household and family evidence; census states Esco’s relation to head, not to Laura.","records":["census1880"],"refs":"C-191; S-1292"},{"id":"john-ellzey-laura","from":"john-ellzey","to":"laura","kind":"parent","label":"John Ellzey → Laura","qualification":"C-213 supported identity; two childhood placements and exact-day Bible abstract, with 1900 father-birthplace conflict.","records":["laura","ellzeyBible"],"refs":"C-213; S-1283, S-1826"},{"id":"elizabeth-coney-laura","from":"elizabeth-coney","to":"laura","kind":"parent","label":"Elizabeth → Laura","qualification":"Supported by Bible abstract; Laura’s original Bible leaf unphotographed and Elizabeth’s 1850 age conflicts.","records":["laura","ellzeyBible"],"refs":"C-213; S-1283, S-1826"},{"id":"john-ellzey-elizabeth","from":"john-ellzey","to":"elizabeth-coney","kind":"spouse","label":"John and Elizabeth","qualification":"1823 marriage index and family Bible abstract; original marriage record unreached.","records":["laura"],"refs":"S-1826; C-213"},{"id":"esco-mary-lou","from":"esco","to":"mary-lou","kind":"spouse","label":"Esco and Mary Lou","qualification":"1899 marriage index and household evidence.","records":["escoMarriage"],"refs":"C-208; S-1277, S-1814"},{"id":"esco-lynnie","from":"esco","to":"lynnie-schilling","kind":"spouse","label":"Esco and Lynnie","qualification":"1947 death index names her Esco’s wife; marriage date not established.","records":["escoObit","lynnieMarriage"],"refs":"S-1842"},{"id":"esco-christine","from":"esco","to":"christine","kind":"parent","label":"Esco → Christine","qualification":"Obituary and household evidence name Christine.","records":["escoObit","nursing"],"refs":"C-112; S-1805"},{"id":"mary-lou-christine","from":"mary-lou","to":"christine","kind":"parent","label":"Mary Lou → Christine","qualification":"Mary Lou’s contemporary obituary names Mrs. Christine Knight as daughter.","records":["maryObit"],"refs":"C-208; S-1814"},{"id":"christine-charles","from":"christine","to":"charles","kind":"spouse","label":"Christine and Charles","qualification":"1946 marriage index; original return unreached.","records":["marriage"],"refs":"C-113; S-0993"},{"id":"henry-mary-lou","from":"henry-t-fussell","to":"mary-lou","kind":"parent","label":"Henry → Mary Lou","qualification":"Henry’s obituary and the family’s card of thanks name Mrs. E. S. Alford as child.","records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"refs":"C-186; S-1296"},{"id":"eliza-mary-lou","from":"eliza-hobbs","to":"mary-lou","kind":"parent","label":"Eliza → Mary Lou","qualification":"Supported by derivative death indexes; one sibling’s index names Sara Jane Hobs instead.","records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"refs":"C-186; S-1277"},{"id":"john-fussell-henry","from":"john-fussell","to":"henry-t-fussell","kind":"parent","label":"John → Henry","qualification":"Death index names father; Harvey in 1860 = Henry in 1870 is inferred.","records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"refs":"C-187; S-1277, S-1278"},{"id":"sarah-corkern-henry","from":"sarah-corkern","to":"henry-t-fussell","kind":"parent","label":"Sarah → Henry","qualification":"Death index names mother; Harvey in 1860 = Henry in 1870 is inferred.","records":["maryObit","fussellFamily"],"refs":"C-187; S-1277, S-1278"},{"id":"jeptha-sarah","from":"jeptha-m-alford","to":"sarah-alford-brumfield","kind":"association","label":"Brother and sister","qualification":"Jeptha’s sworn 1929 affidavit and a contemporary death notice state sibling relationship.","records":["parentage","sarahPension"],"refs":"S-1853"},{"id":"barksdale-john","from":"barksdale-brumfield","to":"john","kind":"association","label":"Nearby patentees","qualification":"Patent geography only; same-day 1858 entries do not establish any family tie.","records":["patents"],"refs":"S-1810"}],"features":[{"id":"jacob-settlement-claim-survey","title":"Jacob’s settlement by the Bogue Chitto","dek":"A dated settlement became a federal claim and, later, a survey for heirs.","body":["Cosby’s 1813 list records a Jacob Alford claim ‘by settlement’ beginning in December 1807. The date anchors the claim beside the Bogue Chitto River, in territory then governed by Spain. It need not be the day Jacob first entered the region. Congress’s 1819 law provided donation grants for qualifying claimants or their legal representatives on the commissioners’ lists; the record does not show an earlier Spanish permit for Jacob.","On 20 January 1831, a survey measured 636.54 acres for the ‘Heirs Jacob Alford.’ The drawing puts the river on the tract’s eastern side and the present Louisiana–Mississippi boundary at its north. An 1851 township plat identifies the same claim by Cosby number 987 and certificate 41. The heirs are not individually named, and successive survey measurements are not separate family grants.","The claimant is probably connected to the father of John S. Alford and his twin, but the survey does not establish that identity or connect him to Julius Alford’s North Carolina son. What can this land record tell us before we connect the men named Jacob?"],"people":["jacob-claimant","john"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"records":["settlement","survey","plat","frontier"],"passages":["river","survey","heirs"],"imageRecord":"survey","refs":["S-1135","S-1227","S-1798","atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §1"]},{"id":"john-field-cemeteries","title":"The field and the two cemeteries","dek":"A field on an 1851 map, John’s later land, and two distinct burial grounds.","body":["A government township map approved in 1851 marks a FIELD along the older Elisha Roberts claim. The drawn field extends into lot 1 of section 18. Seven years later John S. Alford received federal title to that 36.60-acre lot and two lots in section 17, totaling 93.84 acres. The map shows worked ground before John’s patent; it does not say who worked it. A farm return associated with John reports corn, oats and forty improved acres in 1850, but it does not locate that farm on this field.","The mapped Alford family cemetery falls inside the section 18 lot on a modern reconstruction of the patent. That placement does not tell us when the burial ground was established. Laura Ellzey Alford’s weathered marker belongs to this family ground. Bogue Chitto church cemetery is a separate place: Esco’s 1958 death notice explicitly gives his interment there, adjacent to the church.","Modern parcel outlines and the old plat are approximate; neither places John’s house. What does the field actually prove about his farm?"],"people":["john","laura","esco"],"places":["bogue-chitto","family-cemetery","church-cemetery"],"records":["field","patents","farm","laura","escoObit","landscape"],"passages":["field","john-land","laura-death","esco-death"],"imageRecord":"field","refs":["S-1810","S-1670","S-1612","atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§3, 5","S-1857","S-1866"]},{"id":"margaret-church-life","title":"Margaret’s life in three churches","dek":"Her 1885 obituary follows a Methodist profession and two Baptist congregations.","body":["Margaret Alford’s obituary appeared in the 1885 minutes of the Magee’s Creek Baptist Association. It says she first professed religion with the Methodists in 1853. She was baptized into Mt. Pisgah Baptist Church in 1866 and, soon after Bogue Chitto church was constituted, moved her membership there. The text describes a sequence of congregations over more than thirty years, rather than a single church affiliation.","The same obituary reports that Margaret was born in Washington Parish on 9 February 1819, married John S. Alford in 1833, and died on 8 September 1885. It names a husband, fourteen children and forty-three grandchildren among those mourning her. Those numbers describe survivors at her death, not a count of all children born. The account was written decades after her birth and marriage, and it calls her Margaret Alford without giving a maiden name.","Her obituary lets us see how her church remembered her. What part of Margaret’s life does this later church account document directly?"],"people":["margaret","john"],"places":["bogue-chitto"],"records":["margaret"],"passages":["marriage","margaret"],"imageRecord":"margaret","refs":["S-1869","S-1826"]},{"id":"seaborn-service-parole","title":"Seaborn’s service and parole","dek":"Two compiled military cards place him at Camp Moore and Port Hudson.","body":["A Confederate muster card records S. L. Alford’s enlistment at Camp Moore on 13 May 1862, in Company K of the 9th Battalion Louisiana Partisan Rangers. The printed note says that battalion later became Wingfield’s 3rd Louisiana Cavalry. Seaborn L. Alford was John and Margaret’s son, born in 1844; he should not be confused with John’s twin brother Seaborn J., born in 1807.","Another card places S. L. Alford’s capture at Port Hudson on 9 July 1863. It summarizes a roll of prisoners paroled there on 12 and 13 July. The card does not say which of those two days was his exact parole date. Both cards are later abstracts drawn from military rolls, not the rolls themselves.","Period photographs show fortifications and Union artillery at Port Hudson, but none identifies Seaborn or his unit in the scene. The service cards support the enlistment and capture sequence. How much of his personal wartime experience can they reveal?"],"people":["seaborn","john","margaret"],"places":["port-hudson"],"records":["muster","parole","portHudsonWorks","portHudsonBattery"],"passages":["war","port-hudson"],"imageRecord":"parole","refs":["S-1808","S-1809"]},{"id":"farm-household-marriages","title":"Marriages and the farm household","dek":"Seaborn and Laura’s family led to Esco and Mary Lou’s separate household.","body":["A later obituary dates Seaborn L. Alford’s marriage to Laura A. Ellzy to 24 December 1866 and says they had ten children. Esco, born in 1878, appears as a young son in their 1880 household. Laura’s identification as a daughter of John and Elizabeth Ellzey is supported but not established by the material reviewed for the story.","A compiled marriage index gives Esco’s marriage to Mary L. Fussell on 28 December 1899. In the 1900 Washington Parish census, Esco and Mary Lou form a separate household, and Esco is listed as owner of a farm. His father and a brother appear nearby in the census taker’s sequence. That suggests proximity, but the census does not measure the distance between their homes or locate Esco’s farm.","Mary Lou and Esco became Christine’s parents. After Mary Lou’s death in February 1946, the compiled index records two later marriages for Esco; those dates await the original returns. What changes when we follow households rather than assume one continuous family farm?"],"people":["seaborn","laura","esco","mary-lou","christine"],"places":["bogue-chitto","mount-hermon"],"records":["seabornObit","laura","escoMarriage","census1900","maryObit"],"passages":["laura","esco","mary-lou","households","mary-lou-death"],"refs":["S-1292","S-1842","S-1828","C-186"]},{"id":"three-seaborns","title":"Three men named Seaborn","dek":"The twins’ reversed names and a younger namesake span two generations.","body":["An 1884 Methodist obituary describes twin brothers born on 11 October 1807 near present-day Osyka: John Seaborn Alford and Seaborn John Alford. John S. is the man who later married Margaret and acquired Washington Parish land. His twin Seaborn J. became a Methodist local preacher and died in 1884. The obituary’s recollections of their birth and their father Jacob were written many decades later.","The third man, Seaborn L. Alford, was born in May 1844. Evidence from John and Margaret’s household, a family Bible copy and a later naming account supports identifying him as their son. His Confederate service cards place him at Camp Moore in 1862 and Port Hudson in 1863. His 1919 obituary names Laura A. Ellzy as his first wife; Esco appears as their young son in the 1880 household.","The shared name is no proof that the twins’ father was the Jacob of every earlier record. The 1880 entries even disagree on the parents’ birthplaces: John’s entry gives North Carolina; his twin’s gives Virginia. Which date and role separates each Seaborn?"],"people":["john","seaborn","margaret","laura","esco","seaborn-j-twin"],"places":["bogue-chitto","port-hudson"],"records":["twin","parentage","census1880","muster","parole","seabornObit"],"passages":["twins","son","war","seaborn-death"],"imageRecord":"twin","refs":["C-140","S-1134","S-1808","S-1809","S-1612"]},{"id":"esco-brings-christine-home","title":"Esco brings Christine home","dek":"One newspaper column records both her nursing graduation and his trip to New Orleans.","body":["In the Mount Hermon news column of The Bogalusa News for 29 August 1941, Christine Alford’s graduation from Touro Infirmary’s school of nursing is dated 20 August. The item says she was home for a three-week vacation and would return to nurse at the hospital. Its next item reports that Esco Alford drove to New Orleans on Wednesday to accompany her home. Together the two notices connect her new work in the city with a visit to her family community.","The first notice calls her father ‘Asco’; the following notice prints ‘Esco.’ Their placement in one column, and the named Christine Alford in each, support reading them as the same family trip while preserving both spellings. The paper does not describe the route, the vehicle or what father and daughter said on the journey.","A photograph of Touro around 1910 shows the institution decades before Christine attended. It is context, not a picture of her graduating class. What do these two small notices add to the graduation date alone?"],"people":["esco","christine","mary-lou"],"places":["touro","mount-hermon"],"records":["nursing","touroPhoto"],"passages":["touro","graduation"],"imageRecord":"nursing","refs":["S-1017","C-112","The Bogalusa News, 29 August 1941, printed p. 10, ‘Mt. Hermon Events’"]},{"id":"christine-public-health","title":"Christine’s public-health work","dek":"Directories, a census entry and a later obituary trace a long nursing career.","body":["Christine trained at Touro Infirmary in New Orleans and graduated on 20 August 1941, according to a contemporary newspaper notice. It expected her to return there as a hospital nurse after a short visit home. By 1942, the Baton Rouge city directory listed her as a nurse for the Parish Health Unit; the 1943 directory did so again. Those entries document a move into public-health work, though they do not describe her daily duties.","In the 1950 census, Christine Knight was recorded in Baton Rouge as a registered nurse in parish health, working a forty-hour week for government. Her husband Charles was an assistant manager at a funeral home. The adult entries are shown separately because the full household page contains a living descendant.","Christine’s later obituary says she coordinated clinics for children with disabilities across several parishes, served thirty-three years with the Louisiana Public Health Department and retired in 1975. Those broader career details come from the retrospective notice. What parts of her work can contemporary records verify, and what do we know through the later account?"],"people":["christine","charles","esco","mary-lou"],"places":["touro","baton-rouge","mount-hermon"],"records":["nursing","directory","census1950","memorial"],"passages":["touro","baton-rouge","adult-household","retirement"],"imageRecord":"nursing","refs":["S-1017","S-1256","S-1861","C-112","C-184"]}],"places":[{"id":"tar-river","name":"Tar River area, North Carolina","role":"Julius’s family land; Jacob’s documented inheritance","body":["Julius Alford’s 1768 will left son Jacob the home plantation on both sides of the river, whose faint manuscript name is transcribed ‘[Tarr?].’ Jacob’s 1789 deed explicitly calls him Julius’s son. Later deeds concern inherited Tar River land. The regional marker shows a broad area, not the plantation or a house."],"people":["julius-bute","jacob-north-carolina"],"records":["will","deed","ncMove","landscape"],"passages":["north-carolina","will","deed"],"refs":["S-1130","S-1711","S-1131","S-1536"]},{"id":"cumberland","name":"Cumberland County, North Carolina","role":"Jacob’s recorded residence by 1790; later family account of a second marriage","body":["A December 1790 deed calls Julius’s son Jacob a Cumberland County resident while he sold inherited Tar River land. A Cumberland Jacob bought tracts in 1791 and sold them in 1795; identifying him as the same man is probable. Later family histories place a marriage to Franky, or Frances Seaborn, here around 1792, but no confirming marriage record was reviewed. The map uses a county-area marker, not a home site."],"people":["jacob-north-carolina"],"records":["ncMove","jacobFamily","landscape"],"passages":["cumberland","jacob-family"],"refs":["S-1536","S-1608","S-1743","S-1746","S-1563","S-1562"]},{"id":"georgia","name":"Montgomery County, Georgia","role":"Probable stage in Jacob’s southward movement","body":["A Jacob Alford carried a surveyor’s chain here in 1797, appeared in the 1798 tax digest with land and two enslaved people, and served on juries in 1806. Those records establish the Georgia man’s presence, without saying where he came from or went. His identification with the North Carolina and Louisiana men remains probable. The map pin marks a modern county area, not a residence."],"people":["jacob-north-carolina","jacob-claimant"],"records":["georgia","landscape"],"passages":["south-carolina"],"refs":["C-145","C-149","C-140","S-1703","S-1160","S-1699","S-1613"]},{"id":"bogue-chitto","name":"Bogue Chitto River area","role":"Land-claim landscape and later family community","body":["Jacob Alford’s settlement claim begins in December 1807 beside the Bogue Chitto, then in Spanish West Florida. The 1831 survey for his heirs measures 636.54 acres; later John S. and Seaborn L. received separate land grants elsewhere in the township. The mapped outlines compare records from different dates. They do not show one inherited farm, continuous ownership or the positions of family houses."],"people":["jacob-claimant","john","margaret","seaborn","laura","esco","mary-lou"],"records":["settlement","survey","patents","homestead","landscape"],"passages":["river","survey","john-land","households"],"refs":["S-1135","S-1798","S-1670","atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §§1–5"]},{"id":"family-cemetery","name":"Alford family cemetery","role":"Family burial ground within John’s later section 18 lot","body":["The mapped Alford Cemetery lies within the reconstructed lot 1 of section 18 that John S. Alford acquired in 1858. Laura’s weathered marker is in this family ground; a 1965 transcription supplies dates the photo cannot reliably show. The map placement does not date the cemetery’s establishment or identify John’s house. This ground is distinct from the cemetery beside Bogue Chitto church."],"people":["john","laura","seaborn","margaret"],"records":["field","patents","laura","landscape"],"passages":["john-land","laura-death"],"refs":["S-1810","S-1670","S-1857","S-1866","atlas/PLAT-READINGS.md §5","atlas/TOPO-READINGS.md"]},{"id":"church-cemetery","name":"Bogue Chitto church cemetery","role":"Church burial ground, separate from the Alford family cemetery","body":["A 1946 Mt. Hermon notice reports Mrs. Esco Alford’s interment at Bogue Chitto Cemetery; identification as Mary Lou rests on her separate obituary and household evidence. Esco’s 1958 death notice says he was buried in the cemetery adjacent to the church. This burial ground is separate from the Alford family cemetery in Section 18."],"people":["mary-lou","esco"],"records":["maryObit","escoObit"],"passages":["mary-lou-death","esco-death"],"refs":["S-1803","S-1814"]},{"id":"port-hudson","name":"Port Hudson, Louisiana","role":"Seaborn L.’s recorded capture and parole site","body":["Seaborn L. Alford’s compiled military card gives Port Hudson as his place of capture on 9 July 1863. It summarizes a prisoner roll of paroles on 12 and 13 July, without fixing his individual day. The displayed siege photographs show fortifications or Union forces; they do not show Seaborn or prove where within the site he served."],"people":["seaborn"],"records":["parole","muster","portHudsonWorks","portHudsonBattery"],"passages":["war","port-hudson"],"refs":["S-1808","S-1809"]},{"id":"touro","name":"Touro Infirmary, New Orleans","role":"Christine’s nursing school and reported first hospital work","body":["The 29 August 1941 Mount Hermon newspaper column dates Christine’s graduation from Touro’s nursing school to 20 August and says she would return after a three-week visit home to nurse there. A photograph of the Prytania Street institution dates to about 1910. It shows the hospital before Christine’s time, not her class or the particular scene of graduation."],"people":["christine","esco"],"records":["nursing","touroPhoto"],"passages":["touro","graduation"],"refs":["S-1017","C-112","The Bogalusa News, 29 August 1941, printed p. 10"]},{"id":"baton-rouge","name":"Baton Rouge, Louisiana","role":"Christine’s documented public-health work and adult household","body":["Baton Rouge city directories list Christine Alford as a Parish Health Unit nurse in 1942 and 1943. In the 1950 census, Christine Knight is a registered nurse in parish health, while Charles Knight is an assistant manager at a funeral home. These sources place work and residence in the city; the regional map does not trace her exact commute or sites of later clinics."],"people":["christine","charles"],"records":["directory","census1950","memorial"],"passages":["baton-rouge","adult-household","retirement"],"refs":["S-1256","S-1861","C-184"]},{"id":"mount-hermon","name":"Mount Hermon area, Louisiana","role":"Esco and Mary Lou’s family community; Christine’s reported birthplace","body":["Christine’s later obituary says she was born on a farm near Mount Hermon around New Year’s Day 1921. In 1930 she appears with Esco, Lou and brother Tolbert in a farming household. The 1941 Mount Hermon column reports her nursing graduation and Esco’s trip to bring her home from New Orleans. Mary Lou died at home near Mount Hermon in 1946. These records do not locate a specific house."],"people":["esco","mary-lou","christine"],"records":["memorial","census1930","nursing","maryObit"],"passages":["christine","census","graduation","mary-lou-death"],"refs":["S-1017","S-1803","S-1814","C-112","C-208"]}]};
